<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Onn Jaafar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Onn bin Jaafar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Onn Jaafar was a Malay politician and chief minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> who led the opposition to the Malayan Union and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-malays-national-organisation">United Malays National Organisation</a> (UMNO) in 1946. He left UMNO in 1951 after failing to open it to non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a> and founded the Independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> Party, which made little headway. His son Hussein Onn later became prime minister of Malaysia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/onn-jaafar.jpg" alt="Onn Jaafar" width="189" height="287" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The National Archives UK · No restrictions · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CO_1069-504-04_(7893277884)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
